\section{Post a Constraint Before Choosing Its Values}

In logic programming, unification can equate two terms containing unknowns. Constraint logic programming (CLP) generalizes the idea: a domain solver maintains relationships that may hold for many assignments. In CLP over finite integer domains, CLP(FD), \verb|B #>= A + 2| constrains two unknown issue times without choosing either one. In SWI-Prolog, \verb|B is A + 2| instead evaluates the right-hand side now and requires it to be instantiated. That is not a drop-in replacement for a relational schedule.

A constraint store denotes a set of allowed assignments. Adding a constraint intersects that set with another one, so information increases in the reverse-inclusion order even though the set of possibilities shrinks. A propagator can narrow variable domains without enumerating full assignments. Consistency of all the current domains need not establish that the whole constraint store is satisfiable; search may still expose a contradiction. When Prolog backtracks over a choice, branch-local constraints and their consequences are undone. That operation is not monotone growth of a shared Datalog relation.

\section{Schedule a Four-Instruction Basic Block}

Take a toy single-issue target with unit issue occupancy. The four operations are \(A\), a load; \(B\), an independent constant; \(C\), an add that consumes both; and \(D\), the return using the add. \(A\)'s result is available two cycles after it issues; \(B\) and \(C\) each have one-cycle result latency. There are no other dependencies. Wilken, Liu, and Heffernan motivate this style of modeling with dependence graphs and target restrictions, but their paper uses \emph{integer programming}, not the CLP(FD) solver used here. The following is executable SWI-Prolog with its \texttt{library(clpfd)}:

\begin{verbatim}
:- use_module(library(clpfd)).

schedule(LoadLat, Last, [A,B,C,D]) :-
    [A,B,C,D] ins 0..Last,
    all_distinct([A,B,C,D]),
    C #>= A + LoadLat,
    C #>= B + 1,
    D #>= C + 1.

solve(LoadLat, Last, Slots) :-
    schedule(LoadLat, Last, Slots),
    labeling([ffc], Slots).
\end{verbatim}

Use nonnegative integer \texttt{Last} and positive integer \texttt{LoadLat} as inputs. \verb|?- solve(2, 3, Slots).| has the unique solution \verb|Slots = [0,1,2,3]|. At horizon \(3\), the return forces \(C\leq 2\); the latency and nonnegative start times then force \(C=2\), \(A=0\). The issue and data constraints leave cycle \(1\) for \(B\). If the load latency becomes \(3\), \verb|?- solve(3, 3, Slots).| is false: \(C\geq 3\) and \(D\geq 4\). Horizon \(4\) admits exactly \verb|[0,1,3,4]| and \verb|[0,2,3,4]|, irrespective of enumeration order. The gap is a permitted idle cycle.

\Cref{fig:visual-347} distinguishes a latency constraint from an occupied issue slot: \(B\) issues while the load result is still pending.

